(7. Elegance and intelligence are anticorrelated) Tj
T*
(Source: seangoedecke.com RSS feed | Published: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:00:00 GMT) Tj
T*
(Link: https://seangoedecke.com/elegance-and-intelligence-are-anticorrelated/) Tj
T*
(Elegance and intelligence can have a surprisingly negative correlation. Intelligent people) Tj
T*
(sometimes produce inelegant solutions because they?re capable of rapidly brute-forcing) Tj
T*
(problems. If I had to remember a hundred names, I would spend time coming up with clever) Tj
T*
(mnemonics. John von Neumann would probably just remember them. There?s a famous von Neumann) Tj
T*
(story where he was asked how far a fly must travel if it kept flying between two cyclists who) Tj
T*
(were approaching each other at some fixed speed. The trick to the puzzle was to notice that the) Tj
T*
(cyclists would take an hour to meet, so the fly must be travelling for an hour: given the fly?s) Tj
T*
(speed, it?s then easy to calculate the total distance. When von Neumann solved the puzzle) Tj
T*
(instantly, the questioner assumed he?d heard the trick before, but in fact he had just summed) Tj
T*
(the infinite series. If you can do that instantly in your head, why bother with the clever) Tj
T*
(trick? For software engineers, the most important kind of raw intelligence is often the size of) Tj
T*
(your working memory: how many moving pieces you can hold in your head at a given time. Software) Tj
T*
(design involves finding elegant ways to reduce that number of moving pieces, typically...) Tj
